= The Gathering Storm: Economic Distress & the "Committed" State

The euphoric high of the 1971 Bangladesh victory and the socialist slogan *"Garibi Hatao"* evaporated rapidly within two years. By 1973, India was plunged into acute macroeconomic distress, rampant inflation, widespread agrarian unrest, and a ferocious institutional confrontation between the executive branch and the higher judiciary.

== Macroeconomic Headwinds (1972–1975)

The Indian economy suffered a convergence of domestic and global shocks between 1972 and 1975:

- *The Post-War Fiscal Burden:* Financing the 1971 military campaign against Pakistan and providing food, medical aid, and shelter to nearly 10 million Bengali refugees severely depleted India's foreign exchange reserves and national capital budget.
- *Termination of US Aid:* In retaliation against India's military liberation of Bangladesh and the signing of the Indo-Soviet Treaty of Friendship, the United States suspended all foreign developmental aid and stopped grain supplies under Public Law 480 (PL-480).
- *The 1973 Global Oil Shock:* Following the Arab-Israeli October War of 1973, the Organization of the Petroleum Exporting Countries (OPEC) quadrupled international crude oil prices. India's oil import bill ballooned overnight, draining scarce foreign exchange.
- *Hyper-Inflation & Agrarian Collapse:* Consecutive monsoon failures in 1972–1973 caused agricultural output to drop sharply, leading to food scarcity, urban rationing, and speculative hoarding. General wholesale prices escalated by *23% in 1973* and skyrocketed by *30% in 1974*, triggering acute popular immiseration.
- *Industrial Stagnation & Wage Freezes:* Industrial production stalled, factory lay-offs mounted, and the Union government introduced the Additional Emoluments (Compulsory Deposit) Act, effectively freezing the salaries and dearness allowances (DA) of government employees.

== The All-India Railway Strike (May 1974)

Labor unrest reached a climactic showdown in May 1974 with the historic All-India Railway Strike:

- *Leadership and Demands:* The National Coordination Committee for Railwaymen's Struggle (NCCRS), led by the dynamic socialist trade union leader *George Fernandes*, served a charter of demands demanding bonus payments, an 8-hour workday, and parity in wages and benefits with employees of other public sector undertakings.
- *Paralysis of the National Arteries:* When negotiations collapsed, railway workers walked out on 8 May 1974, completely paralysing India's vast freight and passenger transport grid for *20 days*.
- *Executive Suppression:* Viewing the strike as an attempt to destabilise the constitutional government, Prime Minister Indira Gandhi refused to negotiate. She declared the strike illegal, deployed territorial army battalions and the Border Security Force (BSF), carried out preventive arrests of over 30,000 union workers, evicted families from railway quarters, and crushed the strike without conceding a single substantive demand.

== The Judicial Clash & The Doctrine of the "Committed" Judiciary

Simultaneously, a bitter ideological war erupted between the Union government and the Supreme Court over the limits of parliamentary power and fundamental rights:

- *The Underlying Dispute:* Indira Gandhi's government asserted that Parliament, as the elected representative of popular sovereignty, possessed an unfettered right to amend any provision of the Constitution to implement Directive Principles of State Policy and socialist reforms. The Supreme Court insisted that constitutional limits, fundamental rights, and the rule of law must prevail over transient parliamentary majorities.
- *The Precedent Battles:*
  - *Golaknath v. State of Punjab (1967):* An 11-judge bench held that Parliament had no power to abridge Fundamental Rights under Article 368.
  - *RC Cooper (Bank Nationalisation) & Madhav Rao Scindia (Privy Purses) Cases (1970):* The Court invalidated the executive takeovers and derecognition of princely titles, protecting private property.
  - *Kesavananda Bharati v. State of Kerala (24 April 1973):* In the most momentous ruling in Indian jurisprudence, a 13-judge bench held by a narrow 7:6 majority that while Parliament can amend any article of the Constitution, it cannot alter or destroy its *"Basic Structure"*.
- *The Supersession of Judges (25 April 1973):*
  - By established constitutional convention, the senior-most sitting judge of the Supreme Court was invariably elevated as the Chief Justice of India (CJI).
  - When CJI S.M. Sikri retired on 25 April 1973, the Indira Gandhi government broke this sacred convention. It superseded the three senior-most judges who had ruled against the government in the *Kesavananda Bharati* majority—*Justice J.M. Shelat*, *Justice K.S. Hegde*, and *Justice A.N. Grover*.
  - The government appointed *Justice A.N. Ray*, who had authored a minority judgment favoring unfettered parliamentary power, as the new Chief Justice. The three superseded judges resigned immediately in protest.
- *The Ideology of Commitment:* Senior cabinet ministers, notably Mohan Kumaramangalam and H.R. Gokhale, openly justified the action by arguing that the judiciary and the civil service must not be politically neutral referees, but must be *"committed"* to the socialist philosophy and developmental agenda of the ruling party.

