= The Emergency Regime (1975–1977): Dimensions and Excesses

The declaration of Emergency suspended constitutional democracy and concentrated total dictatorial power in the Union executive for 21 months.

== The Repressive Constitutional Machinery

1. *Suspension of Fundamental Rights:* Under Article 359, the President suspended the right of citizens to approach the courts for the enforcement of Fundamental Rights, including Article 14 (Equality), Article 19 (Freedoms of speech, assembly, association), and Article 21 (Protection of life and personal liberty).
2. *Preventive Detention & MISA:* The *Maintenance of Internal Security Act (MISA)* and the Conservation of Foreign Exchange and Prevention of Smuggling Activities Act (COFEPOSA) were weaponised to detain political opponents without charge, warrant, or trial. Nearly 111,000 individuals—including JP, Morarji Desai, Atal Bihari Vajpayee, L.K. Advani, Charan Singh, and George Fernandes—were imprisoned.
3. *Pre-Censorship of the Press:* Electricity to the printing presses of major national dailies along Delhi's Bahadur Shah Zafar Marg was abruptly severed on the night of 25 June. Special press censors were installed in newspaper offices. Editors were legally forbidden from publishing any news, editorial, cartoon, or letter critical of the government. Leading dailies such as *The Indian Express* and *The Statesman* registered silent protest by printing blank editorial columns.
4. *Banning of Socio-Political Organisations:* The government banned 26 political and socio-cultural organisations, prominently including the Rashtriya Swayamsevak Sangh (RSS), Jamaat-e-Islami, Anand Marg, and various Naxalite factions. Strikes, picketing, and demonstrations were criminalised.
5. *The Infamous Habeas Corpus Verdict (ADM Jabalpur, 1976):* When high courts across India bravely entertained habeas corpus petitions of detainees, the Supreme Court in *ADM Jabalpur v. Shivkant Shukla* (April 1976) held by a 4:1 majority (Chief Justice A.N. Ray, Beg, Sarkaria, and Chandrachud) that under a proclaimed Emergency, no citizen had any locus standi to approach a court to challenge illegal executive detention, even if mala fide. *Justice H.R. Khanna* delivered a historic, solitary dissent defending human freedom, for which he was subsequently superseded for the office of Chief Justice.

== Extra-Constitutional Power: Sanjay Gandhi and the Five-Point Programme

While Indira Gandhi unveiled a populist *Twenty-Point Programme* focusing on land ceilings, rural debt liquidation, and handloom subsidies, real executive power gravitated towards her younger son, *Sanjay Gandhi*:

- *The Power Troika:* Sanjay Gandhi held no official cabinet or parliamentary office, yet functioned as an extra-constitutional authority directing Chief Ministers, police chiefs, and cabinet ministers.
- *The Five-Point Programme:* Sanjay championed family planning, afforestation, literacy, anti-dowry campaigns, and slum clearance.
- *The Coercive Excesses:*
  - *Forced Mass Sterilisations:* Bureaucrats, schoolteachers, and police officers were assigned rigid vasectomy and tubectomy quotas. Millions of poor, illiterate men were rounded up and forcibly sterilised in unhygienic roadside camps across northern India.
  - *Turkman Gate Slum Demolitions (April 1976):* Thousands of impoverished Muslim residents in Old Delhi were brutally evicted, their historic homes demolished with bulldozers, and protestors fired upon by police to clear space for urban beautification.

== Underground Resistance and Cultural Dissent

Contrary to the regime's claims of universal compliance, underground resistance flourished across the nation:

- *The Socialist Underground & Baroda Dynamite Case:* George Fernandes evaded arrest by disguising himself as a Sikh sadhu, operating an extensive underground network that printed secret newsletters (*Satya Samachar*) and smuggled communication abroad. He and 24 others were later arrested in the sensational *Baroda Dynamite Case*, charged with conspiring to blow up government railway tracks and bridges to disrupt state authority.
- *Clandestine Networks:* Rashtriya Swayamsevak Sangh (RSS) cadres under Nana Deshmukh organized an underground Satyagraha movement, distributing cyclostyled pamphlets and helping families of jailed political activists.
- *Literary and Intellectual Protest:* Renowned writers registered public outrage by returning prestigious state honors:
  - Celebrated Kannada novelist and scholar *K. Shivarama Karanth* returned his Padma Bhushan award.
  - Renowned Hindi novelist *Phanishwar Nath 'Renu'* returned his Padma Shri in protest against the throttling of democracy.
- *Foreign Media & Diaspora Resistance:* When domestic news was censored, foreign news agencies (BBC, Time, Newsweek) reported regime abuses. Indian diaspora groups organized protests in London and Washington.

== The 42nd Constitutional Amendment Act, 1976: The "Mini-Constitution"

Capitalising on the imprisonment of opposition MPs, the Indira government enacted the sweeping 42nd Constitutional Amendment Act in November 1976:

- *Preamble Alteration:* Inserted the words *"Socialist, Secular"* and *"Integrity"* into the Preamble of the Constitution.
- *Extension of Terms:* Extended the duration of the Lok Sabha and State Legislative Assemblies from *5 years to 6 years*.
- *Subordination of Fundamental Rights:* Made all Directive Principles superior to Fundamental Rights under Articles 14, 19, and 31.
- *Curtailing Judicial Review:* Stripped the Supreme Court and High Courts of their power to evaluate the constitutional validity of constitutional amendment acts.
- *Fundamental Duties:* Added Part IV-A (Article 51A) prescribing ten Fundamental Duties of citizens upon the recommendation of the Swaran Singh Committee.

= The 1977 General Elections & The Janata Restoration

In a shocking and unexpected announcement on 18 January 1977, Prime Minister Indira Gandhi dissolved the Lok Sabha and scheduled general elections for March 1977, releasing political prisoners and easing press censorship.

== Formation of the Janata Party

Sensing a historic opportunity, major non-communist opposition parties submerged their separate identities under the moral guidance of Jayaprakash Narayan to form a single unified political formation: the *Janata Party*.

- *Constituent Parties:* The Janata Party merged four diverse political streams:
  1. *Congress (O)* led by Morarji Desai.
  2. *Bharatiya Jana Sangh (BJS)* led by Atal Bihari Vajpayee and L.K. Advani.
  3. *Bharatiya Lok Dal (BLD)* led by Chaudhary Charan Singh.
  4. *Socialist Party* led by George Fernandes and Madhu Limaye.
- *Congress for Democracy (CFD):* On the eve of the election, veteran Congress leader and Dalit icon *Jagjivan Ram*, along with H.N. Bahuguna and Nandini Satpathy, resigned from Indira Gandhi's cabinet and party, founding the Congress for Democracy (CFD), which formed an electoral alliance with the Janata Party.
- *The Election Narrative:* The election became a stark, bipolar moral referendum: *"Democracy versus Dictatorship"*.

== The Tectonic Verdict of March 1977

The March 1977 election results delivered the most devastating electoral defeat in the history of the Indian National Congress:

- *Janata Landslide:* The Janata Party and its allies swept *330 seats* out of 542 in the Lok Sabha. The Janata Party alone secured *295 seats*, winning an absolute majority on its own.
- *Total Decimation of Congress in North India:* In its traditional Hindi heartland, the Congress was utterly annihilated:
  - Congress won *zero seats* in Uttar Pradesh (0/85), Bihar (0/54), Punjab (0/13), and Haryana (0/10).
  - It secured only 1 seat in Rajasthan and 1 seat in Madhya Pradesh.
  - Both *Indira Gandhi* (defeated by Raj Narain in Rae Bareli) and her son *Sanjay Gandhi* (defeated in Amethi) lost their parliamentary seats.
- *The North-South Divide:* Interestingly, Congress maintained its strength in southern India, winning 41 out of 42 seats in Andhra Pradesh and 26 out of 28 in Karnataka, as the coercive excesses of forced sterilisation and slum demolitions had been concentrated primarily in northern states.
- *First Non-Congress Prime Minister:* On 24 March 1977, 81-year-old *Morarji Desai* was sworn in as India's first non-Congress Prime Minister, heading a coalition government that included Deputy PM Charan Singh and External Affairs Minister Atal Bihari Vajpayee.

== The Fragility of Coalition Rule & The Return of Congress (1977–1980)

Although the Janata Party achieved a historic democratic restoration, its government was crippled by bitter internal rivalries:

- *The Leadership Troika Rivalry:* Intense factional warfare erupted among three competing leaders:
  1. *Morarji Desai:* The ascetic Gandhian Prime Minister representing the conservative Congress (O).
  2. *Chaudhary Charan Singh:* Deputy PM and Home Minister representing the rising agrarian peasant castes (OBCs) of northern India.
  3. *Jagjivan Ram:* Defense Minister and senior Dalit stalwart of the CFD.
- *The Dual-Membership Controversy:* Socialists led by Madhu Limaye objected to former Jana Sangh members (Vajpayee, Advani) maintaining active dual membership in the RSS.
- *Collapse of the Government (July 1979):* Charan Singh broke away from the Janata Party, forcing Morarji Desai's resignation. Charan Singh was sworn in as Prime Minister with outside support from Indira Gandhi's Congress.
- *Charan Singh's Short Premiership:* Indira Gandhi abruptly withdrew support before Charan Singh could even face a single confidence vote on the floor of the Lok Sabha, forcing fresh elections in January 1980.
- *The 1980 Landslide:* Disillusioned by Janata infighting, Indian voters swept Indira Gandhi back to power with 353 Lok Sabha seats in January 1980 on the slogan *"Vote for Indira, Bring Back Stability"*.

= Lessons of the Emergency & Rise of Civil Liberties

The 21-month Emergency was the most severe stress-test ever endured by the Indian democratic republic, yielding enduring institutional safeguards:

== The Shah Commission of Inquiry (May 1977)

The Janata government appointed a commission of inquiry headed by retired Supreme Court Chief Justice *J.C. Shah*:

- *The Mandate:* To investigate allegations of abuse of authority, excesses, illegal arrests, custodial tortures, and bulldozing committed during the Emergency.
- *Key Findings:* The Shah Commission uncovered that thousands were jailed without legal grounds; the Cabinet was unconstitutionally bypassed during the midnight declaration; and Sanjay Gandhi operated as an illegal power center without democratic accountability.

== The 44th Constitutional Amendment Act, 1978

To permanently inoculate the Indian Constitution against future authoritarian subversion, the Janata government passed the 44th Constitutional Amendment Act, 1978:

- *Grounds for Emergency:* Substituted the vague phrase *"internal disturbance"* with *"armed rebellion"*.
- *Cabinet Written Advice:* Mandated that the President can proclaim an Emergency *only* upon the unanimous *written advice* of the Union Cabinet, eliminating midnight unilateral decisions by a Prime Minister.
- *Parliamentary Approval:* Required parliamentary approval by a special two-thirds majority within one month (instead of two months by simple majority).
- *Non-Suspendable Rights:* Explicitly stipulated that the fundamental rights enshrined in *Article 20* (Protection against retroactive criminal laws and double jeopardy) and *Article 21* (Right to life and personal liberty) *cannot be suspended* under any circumstances, even during a proclaimed Emergency.
- *Restoration of Legislative Terms:* Restored the regular 5-year term for the Lok Sabha and State Legislative Assemblies.

== The Modern Human Rights Awakening & PIL Revolution

The state terror of the Emergency catalyzed India's modern civil liberties movement. Realising that institutional checks could crumble, citizens founded autonomous watchdog organisations, prominently the *People's Union for Civil Liberties (PUCL)* and the *People's Union for Democratic Rights (PUDR)*. 

Furthermore, to regain the public trust lost during the ADM Jabalpur ruling, the Supreme Court in the late 1970s and 1980s under *Justices P.N. Bhagwati* and *V.R. Krishna Iyer* pioneered *Public Interest Litigation (PIL)* and relaxed the traditional rules of *locus standi*, transforming the higher judiciary into a champion of the disadvantaged and marginalized.

