= Practice Questions (Exam Pattern)

Fifteen authentic CUET UG examination-standard multiple-choice questions testing conceptual, chronological, institutional, and factual mastery. Marking scheme: $+5$ for correct answer, $-1$ for incorrect attempt.

== Part 1: Economic Strains, Student Movements & Judicial Clash

#mcq(1, [The nationwide 20-day All-India Railway Strike in May 1974 was organized and led by which prominent trade union leader?], (
  [A.K. Gopalan], [George Fernandes], [Morarji Desai], [Charan Singh],
))

#solution(1, [George Fernandes led the National Coordination Committee for Railwaymen’s Struggle in May 1974, bringing the national railway grid to a complete standstill for 20 days.], answer: [B])

#mcq(2, [In April 1973, which judge was appointed as the Chief Justice of India by superseding three senior Supreme Court judges who ruled in the Kesavananda Bharati case?], (
  [Justice A.N. Ray], [Justice J.M. Shelat], [Justice H.R. Khanna], [Justice V.R. Krishna Iyer],
))

#solution(2, [Justice A.N. Ray was elevated as CJI in April 1973 by superseding three senior judges (Shelat, Hegde, Grover) who ruled against unfettered parliamentary power in Kesavananda Bharati.], answer: [A])

#mcq(3, [The Navnirman student movement of January 1974, which erupted over rising hostel mess food bills and corruption, occurred in which state?], (
  [Bihar], [Uttar Pradesh], [Gujarat], [Punjab],
))

#solution(3, [The Navnirman Movement took place in Gujarat in early 1974, triggered by engineering hostel mess fee hikes, leading to Morarji Desai's fast and the dissolution of the state assembly.], answer: [C])

#mcq(4, [Who gave the historic clarion call for "Total Revolution" (Sampoorna Kranti) during the Bihar student agitation of 1974?], (
  [Dr. Rammanohar Lohia], [Jayaprakash Narayan], [Acharya Narendra Deva], [K. Kamaraj],
))

#solution(4, [Jayaprakash Narayan (JP) accepted the leadership of the Bihar student movement and issued the historic call for "Total Revolution" (Sampoorna Kranti) across all walks of society.], answer: [B])

#mcq(5, [On 12 June 1975, which High Court judge invalidated the 1971 election of Prime Minister Indira Gandhi on grounds of corrupt electoral practices in an election petition filed by Raj Narain?], (
  [Justice Jagmohan Lal Sinha of the Allahabad High Court], [Justice P.N. Bhagwati], [Justice J.C. Shah], [Justice H.R. Khanna],
))

#solution(5, [Justice Jagmohan Lal Sinha of the Allahabad High Court delivered the landmark verdict on 12 June 1975 in the election petition filed by socialist leader Raj Narain.], answer: [A])

#pagebreak()
== Part 2: Emergency Dimensions, Excesses & 42nd Amendment

#mcq(6, [The National Emergency on the night of 25 June 1975 was proclaimed by President Fakhruddin Ali Ahmed under which constitutional provision?], (
  [Article 356 on grounds of failure of constitutional machinery], [Article 360 on grounds of financial breakdown], [Article 352 on grounds of "internal disturbance"], [Article 352 on grounds of "armed rebellion"],
))

#solution(6, [The 1975 Emergency was proclaimed under Article 352 on the ground of "internal disturbance". "Armed rebellion" was only substituted later by the 44th Amendment in 1978.], answer: [C])

#mcq(7, [Which major national newspapers chose to register silent resistance against executive pre-censorship during the Emergency by leaving their editorial columns completely blank?], (
  [The Hindu and The Tribune], [The Indian Express and The Statesman], [The Times of India and Hindustan Times], [National Herald and Patriot],
))

#solution(7, [The Indian Express and The Statesman famously registered heroic silent defiance against state censorship by publishing blank editorial columns.], answer: [B])

#mcq(8, [The controversial 42nd Constitutional Amendment Act of 1976 extended the term of the Lok Sabha and State Legislative Assemblies from 5 years to:], (
  [4 years], [7 years], [Indefinite duration], [6 years],
))

#solution(8, [The 42nd Constitutional Amendment Act of 1976 extended the tenure of the Lok Sabha and Vidhan Sabhas from 5 years to 6 years (later restored to 5 years by the 44th Amendment).], answer: [D])

#mcq(9, [In the historic March 1977 General Elections, which newly formed umbrella political party defeated the ruling Congress party to form India's first non-Congress central government?], (
  [Swatantra Party], [Bharatiya Jana Sangh], [Janata Party], [Congress for Democracy],
))

#solution(9, [The Janata Party was formed in early 1977 by the merger of Congress (O), Jana Sangh, Bharatiya Lok Dal, and Socialists, winning 295 seats and sweeping to power under Morarji Desai.], answer: [C])

#mcq(10, [Which Constitutional Amendment Act replaced the vague term "internal disturbance" with "armed rebellion" and prohibited the suspension of Articles 20 and 21 during an Emergency?], (
  [44th Constitutional Amendment Act, 1978], [42nd Constitutional Amendment Act, 1976], [52nd Constitutional Amendment Act, 1985], [38th Constitutional Amendment Act, 1975],
))

#solution(10, [The 44th Constitutional Amendment Act of 1978 enacted by the Janata government introduced vital safeguards, including substituting "armed rebellion" for "internal disturbance" and making Article 20 and 21 inviolable.], answer: [A])

#pagebreak()
== Part 3: Inquiries, Jurisprudence & Electoral Geography

#mcq(11, [Which Commission of Inquiry was appointed by the Janata Party government in May 1977 to investigate allegations of abuse of authority, excesses, and malpractices committed during the Emergency?], (
  [The Sarkaria Commission], [The Mandal Commission], [The Kothari Commission], [The Shah Commission headed by retired Chief Justice J.C. Shah],
))

#solution(11, [The Shah Commission of Inquiry headed by retired Chief Justice J.C. Shah was appointed in May 1977 to investigate administrative abuses and extra-constitutional excesses during the Emergency.], answer: [D])

#mcq(12, [During the 1975–1977 Emergency, hundreds of political leaders and dissidents were arrested without trial under which draconian preventive detention legislation?], (
  [TADA], [POTA], [Maintenance of Internal Security Act (MISA)], [Armed Forces Special Powers Act (AFSPA)],
))

#solution(12, [The Maintenance of Internal Security Act (MISA) was ruthlessly weaponised during the Emergency to detain nearly 1.1 lakh political opponents and activists without trial.], answer: [C])

#mcq(13, [In the infamous ADM Jabalpur v. Shivkant Shukla (1976) case, also known as the Habeas Corpus case, which Supreme Court judge delivered the lone, courageous dissenting opinion defending personal liberty?], (
  [Justice H.R. Khanna], [Justice A.N. Ray], [Justice P.N. Bhagwati], [Justice M.H. Beg],
))

#solution(13, [Justice H.R. Khanna delivered the sole courageous dissent in ADM Jabalpur, ruling that the State cannot deprive a person of life or liberty without the authority of law, even during an Emergency.], answer: [A])

#mcq(14, [Sanjay Gandhi's extra-constitutional 5-point programme implemented aggressively during the Emergency prominently included which controversial initiatives?], (
  [Nationalisation of commercial banks], [Coercive mass sterilisation (vasectomies) and demolition of slums such as at Turkman Gate in Delhi], [Abolition of Privy Purses], [Launch of the Green Revolution],
))

#solution(14, [Sanjay Gandhi's 5-point programme was notorious for coercive mass vasectomies with bureaucratic quotas and the violent demolition of poor slums at Turkman Gate in Old Delhi.], answer: [B])

#mcq(15, [In the 1977 General Elections, what was the performance of the Congress party in the northern Indian states of Uttar Pradesh, Bihar, Punjab, and Haryana?], (
  [Congress retained a slim majority in all four states], [Congress won only half the seats in Uttar Pradesh and Bihar], [Congress improved its performance due to the 20-Point Programme], [Congress was completely wiped out, winning zero seats across all four states, with both Indira Gandhi and Sanjay Gandhi losing their seats],
))

#solution(15, [Congress was completely wiped out in the Hindi heartland, winning zero seats in UP (0/85), Bihar (0/54), Punjab (0/13), and Haryana (0/10), with both Indira and Sanjay Gandhi suffering personal electoral defeats.], answer: [D])

